WEBVTT

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.766
Last video ended on this picture: the natural

00:00:02.806 --> 00:00:05.031
log curve, and log base two of x.

00:00:05.754 --> 00:00:07.199
Logs like these are how we hear.

00:00:07.903 --> 00:00:09.849
Double the frequency of a guitar note, and

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:10.872
it climbs one octave.

00:00:11.556 --> 00:00:13.685
So the octaves you've climbed are log base

00:00:13.706 --> 00:00:15.292
two of the frequency ratio.

00:00:16.197 --> 00:00:18.504
Make a sound ten times as powerful, and

00:00:18.545 --> 00:00:21.052
its sound level rises by one bel, which

00:00:21.113 --> 00:00:22.035
is ten decibels.

00:00:22.780 --> 00:00:24.864
So the rise in bels is log base

00:00:24.905 --> 00:00:26.348
ten of the power ratio.

00:00:27.354 --> 00:00:29.205
Let's add log base ten of x as

00:00:29.246 --> 00:00:29.427
well.

00:00:30.353 --> 00:00:32.476
All three pass through the same point, one,

00:00:32.677 --> 00:00:35.121
zero, because any base to the power zero

00:00:35.282 --> 00:00:35.722
is one.

00:00:36.545 --> 00:00:38.191
But look at how they leave that point.

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:41.260
Log base two climbs the steepest, and log

00:00:41.301 --> 00:00:42.724
base ten is the flattest.

00:00:43.286 --> 00:00:45.548
So how does the base set the slope,

00:00:45.990 --> 00:00:47.451
and by exactly how much?

00:00:53.561 --> 00:00:56.051
Measure it, at the same x each time.

00:00:56.653 --> 00:00:59.079
At x equals one, the natural log curve

00:00:59.099 --> 00:01:01.124
has slope one, from last video.

00:01:01.786 --> 00:01:04.040
Log base two has slope about one point

00:01:04.101 --> 00:01:04.584
four four.

00:01:05.451 --> 00:01:05.995
Now move along.

00:01:06.458 --> 00:01:08.963
At x equals two, the natural log's slope

00:01:09.024 --> 00:01:11.228
is a half, and log base two's is

00:01:11.268 --> 00:01:12.350
about point seven two.

00:01:13.175 --> 00:01:13.957
At x equals four,

00:01:14.481 --> 00:01:16.771
a quarter, against about point three six.

00:01:17.556 --> 00:01:19.400
Divide one slope by the other, and you

00:01:19.441 --> 00:01:20.703
get one point four four,

00:01:21.266 --> 00:01:21.686
every time.

00:01:22.449 --> 00:01:24.313
At the same x, log base two is

00:01:24.354 --> 00:01:26.419
always one point four four times as steep.

00:01:27.076 --> 00:01:28.442
Why one point four four?

00:01:29.329 --> 00:01:30.033
Look at the heights.

00:01:30.678 --> 00:01:32.706
At x equals four, log base two is

00:01:32.788 --> 00:01:35.017
two, because two squared is four.

00:01:35.759 --> 00:01:37.482
The natural log of four is about one

00:01:37.543 --> 00:01:38.403
point three nine,

00:01:38.925 --> 00:01:41.108
and two is one point four four times

00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:41.349
that.

00:01:42.132 --> 00:01:43.938
That's the change of base rule from Year

00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:44.078
11.

00:01:45.163 --> 00:01:47.547
Log base two of x equals the natural

00:01:47.588 --> 00:01:50.032
log of x, divided by the natural log

00:01:50.053 --> 00:01:50.472
of two.

00:01:51.456 --> 00:01:53.581
Dividing by the natural log of two, about

00:01:53.802 --> 00:01:55.947
point six nine three, is the same as

00:01:56.089 --> 00:01:58.896
multiplying by one over it, about one point

00:01:58.957 --> 00:01:59.477
four four.

00:02:00.181 --> 00:02:02.833
That one number is the factor, and every

00:02:02.914 --> 00:02:04.299
height is multiplied by it.

00:02:04.943 --> 00:02:07.188
So log base two is the natural log

00:02:07.229 --> 00:02:08.692
curve, stretched upward.

00:02:09.362 --> 00:02:11.415
A vertical stretch does the same thing to

00:02:11.476 --> 00:02:11.838
slopes.

00:02:12.562 --> 00:02:14.804
Take the tangent triangle on the natural log

00:02:14.844 --> 00:02:16.405
curve at x equals two:

00:02:17.005 --> 00:02:17.445
run two,

00:02:17.966 --> 00:02:18.426
rise one,

00:02:18.966 --> 00:02:19.387
slope a half.

00:02:20.449 --> 00:02:22.877
Stretch it upward by one point four four.

00:02:23.399 --> 00:02:25.787
The run stays two, but the rise becomes

00:02:26.048 --> 00:02:27.011
one point four four.

00:02:27.674 --> 00:02:29.998
So the slope becomes about point seven two,

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.504
which is one point four four times a

00:02:32.565 --> 00:02:32.805
half.

00:02:33.708 --> 00:02:36.093
Every rise at the same x is multiplied

00:02:36.134 --> 00:02:38.439
by the factor, so every slope is too.

00:02:39.221 --> 00:02:41.550
Now the proof, for any base a.

00:02:42.112 --> 00:02:43.640
The base has to be positive, and it

00:02:43.741 --> 00:02:45.430
can't be one, which we'll come back to.

00:02:46.033 --> 00:02:48.198
By the change of base rule, log base

00:02:48.418 --> 00:02:50.423
a of x equals the natural log of

00:02:50.543 --> 00:02:52.226
x, over the natural log of a.

00:02:52.828 --> 00:02:54.574
For a fixed base, the natural log of

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:55.878
a is just a number.

00:02:56.340 --> 00:02:58.444
So this is one over the natural log

00:02:58.466 --> 00:03:00.650
of a, times the natural log of x.

00:03:01.394 --> 00:03:03.539
A constant multiple stays in front when you

00:03:03.560 --> 00:03:05.966
differentiate, and the natural log of x has

00:03:06.007 --> 00:03:08.232
derivative one over x, for positive x.

00:03:08.956 --> 00:03:10.962
So the derivative of log base a of

00:03:11.164 --> 00:03:13.933
x is one over, x times the natural

00:03:13.954 --> 00:03:14.355
log of a.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:15.748
Check base e.

00:03:16.338 --> 00:03:18.330
The natural log of e is one, so

00:03:18.411 --> 00:03:19.638
we get back one over x.

00:03:20.189 --> 00:03:21.113
Try base ten.

00:03:21.818 --> 00:03:23.801
The natural log of ten is about two

00:03:23.842 --> 00:03:24.342
point three,

00:03:24.844 --> 00:03:25.504
so the factor,

00:03:26.046 --> 00:03:27.688
one over the natural log of ten, is

00:03:27.749 --> 00:03:28.970
about point four three.

00:03:29.572 --> 00:03:30.926
That squashes the curve.

00:03:31.814 --> 00:03:34.277
At x equals one, log base ten has

00:03:34.358 --> 00:03:37.122
slope about point four three, flatter than the

00:03:37.163 --> 00:03:37.703
natural log,

00:03:38.225 --> 00:03:39.266
just as the picture showed.

00:03:40.268 --> 00:03:41.748
If you put the natural log of ten

00:03:41.889 --> 00:03:44.050
on top instead, you'd get a slope of

00:03:44.111 --> 00:03:45.390
about two point three,

00:03:45.892 --> 00:03:47.452
steeper than the natural log curve.

00:03:47.913 --> 00:03:50.097
But log base ten is the flattest of

00:03:50.138 --> 00:03:52.261
the three, so the natural log of the

00:03:52.322 --> 00:03:53.945
base has to go underneath.

00:03:54.835 --> 00:03:57.648
Now turn the base, and watch the slope

00:03:57.729 --> 00:03:58.693
at x equals one.

00:03:59.477 --> 00:04:01.264
It's one over the natural log of a.

00:04:02.249 --> 00:04:04.577
At base ten, about point four three.

00:04:05.301 --> 00:04:08.112
At base e, exactly one, and the curve

00:04:08.194 --> 00:04:09.418
is the natural log itself.

00:04:10.202 --> 00:04:12.470
At base two, about one point four four.

00:04:13.213 --> 00:04:15.217
As the base gets close to one, its

00:04:15.338 --> 00:04:17.582
natural log gets close to zero, so the

00:04:17.623 --> 00:04:18.544
slope shoots up.

00:04:19.147 --> 00:04:20.792
At base one point one, it's about ten

00:04:20.833 --> 00:04:21.314
and a half.

00:04:22.017 --> 00:04:24.106
Base one itself is not allowed.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:27.237
One to any power is one, so no

00:04:27.298 --> 00:04:28.781
power of one gives two,

00:04:29.284 --> 00:04:31.209
and log base one doesn't exist.

00:04:31.974 --> 00:04:32.799
The formula agrees:

00:04:33.304 --> 00:04:34.311
it would divide by zero.

00:04:34.775 --> 00:04:37.109
What about a base below one, like a

00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:37.511
half?

00:04:38.317 --> 00:04:40.385
The natural log of a half is negative,

00:04:40.707 --> 00:04:42.634
about minus point six nine three.

00:04:43.298 --> 00:04:45.643
So the factor, one over the natural log

00:04:45.664 --> 00:04:46.124
of a half,

00:04:46.646 --> 00:04:48.930
is about minus one point four four.

00:04:49.754 --> 00:04:52.200
A negative factor stretches the natural log curve

00:04:52.241 --> 00:04:54.789
by one point four four, and also mirrors

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:56.093
it in the x axis.

00:04:56.756 --> 00:04:59.503
So log base a half falls as x

00:04:59.605 --> 00:05:00.206
increases.

00:05:01.029 --> 00:05:03.357
Its slope at every positive x is negative.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:06.146
At x equals one, it's about minus one

00:05:06.207 --> 00:05:07.028
point four four.

00:05:07.772 --> 00:05:10.042
The same formula covers it: the minus sign

00:05:10.124 --> 00:05:11.931
comes from the natural log of the base.

00:05:12.586 --> 00:05:13.098
Let's use it.

00:05:13.821 --> 00:05:17.108
Differentiate y equals log base two of x,

00:05:17.549 --> 00:05:19.913
then find the gradient at x equals four.

00:05:20.716 --> 00:05:22.903
The base is two, so d y by

00:05:22.924 --> 00:05:25.412
d x is one over, x times the

00:05:25.472 --> 00:05:26.395
natural log of two.

00:05:27.118 --> 00:05:30.046
At x equals four, that's one over, four

00:05:30.086 --> 00:05:31.409
times the natural log of two.

00:05:32.033 --> 00:05:33.097
Keep it exact like that,

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:35.548
or round it to about point three six

00:05:35.630 --> 00:05:35.709
one.

00:05:36.494 --> 00:05:38.020
That's the slope we measured at the start.

00:05:38.704 --> 00:05:39.265
On the guitar,

00:05:39.767 --> 00:05:42.231
x equals four is two octaves above the

00:05:42.312 --> 00:05:43.614
open string, at concert A.

00:05:44.498 --> 00:05:44.838
Up there,

00:05:45.341 --> 00:05:47.890
each extra hertz raises the pitch only a

00:05:48.051 --> 00:05:49.576
quarter as much as it does at the

00:05:49.657 --> 00:05:50.138
open string.

00:05:50.999 --> 00:05:51.851
Here's a way to remember it.

00:05:52.397 --> 00:05:54.164
Divide the curve, divide the slope.

00:05:55.027 --> 00:05:57.709
Log base a of x is the natural

00:05:57.750 --> 00:05:59.911
log curve divided by the natural log of

00:05:59.932 --> 00:06:01.432
a, at every x.

00:06:02.074 --> 00:06:05.122
So its slope is one over x, divided

00:06:05.163 --> 00:06:06.686
by the natural log of a too.

00:06:07.508 --> 00:06:08.969
And when the natural log of a is

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:11.871
negative, the division also flips the curve over,

00:06:12.193 --> 00:06:13.313
so the slope turns negative.

00:06:16.038 --> 00:06:16.421
Your turn.

00:06:17.126 --> 00:06:20.172
Differentiate y equals log base five of x.

00:06:20.634 --> 00:06:22.943
Then find the exact gradient of the curve

00:06:23.065 --> 00:06:24.048
at x equals two.

00:06:24.736 --> 00:06:25.647
Pause here to answer.

00:06:36.388 --> 00:06:38.876
The base is five, so d y by

00:06:38.917 --> 00:06:40.321
d x is one over,

00:06:40.844 --> 00:06:42.509
x times the natural log of five.

00:06:43.192 --> 00:06:45.473
At x equals two, the gradient is one

00:06:45.694 --> 00:06:47.915
over, two times the natural log of five,

00:06:48.056 --> 00:06:49.336
about point three one.

00:06:50.098 --> 00:06:51.861
Five is bigger than e, so the curve

00:06:51.902 --> 00:06:53.605
is flatter than the natural log,

00:06:54.127 --> 00:06:55.830
and point three one is less than a

00:06:55.911 --> 00:06:56.191
half.

00:06:56.893 --> 00:06:58.625
The natural log of five on top would

00:06:58.647 --> 00:06:59.774
give about point eight.

00:06:59.937 --> 00:07:00.563
That's too steep.

00:07:02.144 --> 00:07:02.529
Last one.

00:07:03.312 --> 00:07:06.260
For h of x equals log base a

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:08.164
third of x, with x positive,

00:07:08.726 --> 00:07:10.069
find h prime of x.

00:07:10.731 --> 00:07:13.722
Then state whether h is increasing or decreasing,

00:07:14.063 --> 00:07:14.705
and give a reason.

00:07:15.408 --> 00:07:16.393
Pause here to answer.

00:07:28.354 --> 00:07:30.895
The base is a third, so h prime

00:07:30.916 --> 00:07:32.317
of x is one over,

00:07:32.898 --> 00:07:35.079
x times the natural log of a third.

00:07:35.782 --> 00:07:37.966
The natural log of a third is minus

00:07:38.007 --> 00:07:39.871
the natural log of three, which is negative.

00:07:40.614 --> 00:07:42.855
And x is positive, so h prime of

00:07:42.976 --> 00:07:45.757
x is negative for every positive x, and

00:07:45.938 --> 00:07:46.638
h is decreasing.

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:49.084
If you used the natural log of three,

00:07:49.446 --> 00:07:51.470
you lost that minus sign, and the curve

00:07:51.511 --> 00:07:52.352
would seem to rise.

00:07:54.034 --> 00:07:56.697
So changing the base scales the natural log

00:07:56.738 --> 00:07:58.979
curve by one over the natural log of

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:01.482
a, and every slope at the same x

00:08:01.663 --> 00:08:02.363
scales with it.

00:08:03.287 --> 00:08:04.615
Next, we leave logs behind.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:06.112
Here's the sine curve.

00:08:06.962 --> 00:08:08.650
Can you read its gradient straight from the

00:08:08.671 --> 00:08:08.951
graph?

00:08:11.528 --> 00:08:12.391
Here are the key notes.

00:08:12.794 --> 00:08:14.341
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:14.383 --> 00:08:14.844
to copy them.
